  "limitations": [
    "Each OS contributes one native run with three repetitions per case and one HTTP suite with three repeated text requests and one synthetic image. These short runs do not establish a statistically meaningful operating-system performance difference.",
    "Native prompt and generation rates exclude HTTP, the vision projector and model load. HTTP timings use a resident model; first visible output includes prompt processing and local HTTP overhead. Server decode timing is a separate metric.",
    "The seven checks validate the fixed arithmetic, instruction, JSON, completed-text and synthetic-image cases. The three repeated text checks establish completion, not semantic quality or general model accuracy.",
    "All repeated HTTP prompts have 63 prompt tokens and 71 completion tokens on both systems. Temperatures, cache history, compilers, CPU build targets, base services and image features still differ; these are full-stack observations.",
    "Yocto has no swap; Ubuntu reports 2,097,148 KiB of swap and reaches 173 MiB sampled swap use during HTTP. Peak used RAM is whole-system usage, not model allocation or available headroom. MemAvailable snapshots are retained separately and do not reveal the minimum during a run.",
    "The native after-memory snapshot is after the benchmark process exits; the HTTP snapshots retain a resident server. Ubuntu includes its existing ARK-OS/background services. These observations do not prove equal low-memory resilience or isolate OS memory savings.",
    "Telemetry uses nominal one-second samples, which can miss peaks. Counts and clock ranges are retained. Power is sampled module VDD_IN, not external supply power or energy per token; arithmetic means include startup, work and pauses in each capture.",
    "Timed logs were quiet. A separate tiny verbose diagnostic directly confirmed full 65/65 offload and flash attention and recorded graph warmup activity. Its timings are excluded. Compiled graph support and environment policy do not prove graph replay for every measured request.",
    "Model-loading health latency, Linux cold boot and complete application readiness are distinct and are not measured by the inference rates here. No claim is made about long context, concurrent clients, cameras, sustained thermals or production deployment."
  ]
